<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Eastern Buyeo</h1><p class="st-meta">86 BCE – 22 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A branch kingdom of Buyeo near the Korean east coast, remembered in Korean legend as the birthplace of Goguryeo's founder Jumong.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The 12th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a> relates that King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haeburu">Haeburu of Buyeo</a> moved his court east to a place called Gaseopwon on divine advice, founding Eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/buyeo">Buyeo</a>, traditionally in 86 BCE. Its location is uncertain, perhaps in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">Tumen</a> river region. The story is legendary in detail and Chinese sources do not clearly distinguish it from Buyeo itself.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Korean tradition names three kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haeburu">Haeburu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geumwa">Geumwa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daeso">Daeso</a>. In the legend Geumwa took in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuhwa">Yuhwa</a>, mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dongmyeong-of-goguryeo">Jumong</a>, who later fled south to found <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo</a>. Little else is known of its government or society, which presumably resembled that of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/buyeo">Buyeo</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Rivalry with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo</a> ended in war: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daemusin-of-goguryeo">King Daemusin</a> of Goguryeo invaded and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daeso">Daeso</a> was killed in 22 CE, after which members of the royal house submitted to Goguryeo.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/eastern-buyeo.jpg" alt="Eastern Buyeo" width="320" height="501"><figcaption>Image: Asieon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:3rd_century_Buyeo%E2%80%93Eastern_Buyeo.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>86 BCE – 22 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Haeburu, Geumwa, Daeso</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous beliefs</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a>, <a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dongbuyeo, Dong Buyeo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-32&amp;focus=eastern-buyeo" aria-label="Eastern Buyeo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7425 1002 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Eastern Buyeo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7992 862h0M8115 1035h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7745 1258h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7745 1258h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=-32&amp;focus=eastern-buyeo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
